---
import {
  MODE_INFO,
  PLAN_COPY,
  PLAN_IDS,
  PLAN_LIMITS,
  PRODUCT_MODES_OFFERED,
  PRODUCT_MODE_INFO,
  PRODUCT_MODE_TO_SPECIALIST,
  fullRateDays,
  monthlyCeilingBitesFirst,
  type ProductMode,
} from '@golem/shared';

// Read, never restated: this page explains the quota, so a stale copy of it here would be the
// documentation contradicting the thing it documents.
const free = PLAN_LIMITS.free;
const paid = PLAN_IDS.filter((id) => id !== 'free' && PLAN_COPY[id].priceUsdMonthly !== null);
import DocsLayout from '../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import { GENERATED_IMAGE_MAX_BYTES, GENERATED_PROJECT_BYTES, GENERATED_PROJECT_COUNT } from '../../../../worker/src/generated-image-limits';

/*
 * THE PER-MODE PRICES WERE TYPED HERE, AND ONE OF THEM PRICED A MODE NOBODY CAN BUY.
 *
 * Three lines under "The numbers" quoted Plan, Agent and Super Agent, with a surcharge —
 * "+1 per extra step" — worked into the metering paragraph and the thrift advice below it. Super
 * Agent is in PRODUCT_MODES, which is every mode the SYSTEM can produce, and not in
 * PRODUCT_MODES_OFFERED, which is the modes a PERSON may choose. Quoting a price for it is worse
 * than describing it: a price is the most concrete promise a page can make, and this one was
 * attached to a button that does not exist.
 *
 * So the rows are DERIVED, the same way /pricing and /docs/modes derive theirs, through
 * PRODUCT_MODE_TO_SPECIALIST into MODE_INFO. The advice under the callout is keyed by mode for the
 * same reason: a tip for a withdrawn mode cannot be reached, and a mode that gains an offer needs
 * one written before it can be advised about.
 */
const modes = PRODUCT_MODES_OFFERED.map((m) => {
  // "4-18" quotes 4. What a request STARTS at is the comparable number; the paragraph under the
  // list is where the rest of the range is explained, rather than in a figure that looks like two.
  const published = MODE_INFO[PRODUCT_MODE_TO_SPECIALIST[m]].typicalCredits;
  const credits = Number(String(published).split('-')[0]);
  if (!Number.isFinite(credits) || credits <= 0) {
    throw new Error(`credits-and-limits.astro: "${published}" does not start with a Credit count`);
  }
  return {
    name: PRODUCT_MODE_INFO[m].name,
    credits,
    perFreeDay: Math.floor(free.creditsPerDay / credits),
  };
});

/** Keyed, so a clause about a mode nobody is offered cannot be rendered by accident. */
const THRIFT: Partial<Record<ProductMode, string>> = {
  plan: 'design the build in Plan before you run it — a question costs a fraction of a wrong build',
  agent: 'give Agent specific requests (“the door in Workspace.Lobby”) rather than vague ones',
};
const thrift = PRODUCT_MODES_OFFERED.map((m) => THRIFT[m]).filter(Boolean).join('; ');

const sessionShape = modes
  .map((m) => `about ${m.perFreeDay} ${m.name} requests`)
  .join(', or ');
---

<DocsLayout
  title="Credits & limits — Apple Docs"
  description={`How Apple's Credits quota works: ${free.creditsPerDay} per day free, per-mode costs, live metering, daily reset, and exactly what happens when you run out.`}
  heading="Credits &amp; limits"
>
  <p>
    Credits are Apple's unit of daily energy. Every AI request burns some; your balance refills to
    full once a day, <strong>up to a monthly ceiling</strong>. The system exists for one reason:
    real inference costs real compute, and a hard, transparent quota is what lets the free tier be
    genuinely free — no card, no trial clock, no surprise bill.
  </p>

  <h2>The numbers</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Free plan:</strong> {free.creditsPerDay} Credits per day · {free.creditsPerMonth.toLocaleString()} a month.</li>
    {paid.map((id) => (
      <li><strong>{PLAN_COPY[id].name} plan:</strong> {PLAN_LIMITS[id].creditsPerDay} Credits per day · {PLAN_LIMITS[id].creditsPerMonth.toLocaleString()} a month.</li>
    ))}
    {modes.map((m) => (
      <li><strong>{m.name}:</strong> from {m.credits} credits per request.</li>
    ))}
  </ul>
  <p>
    Those are starting prices, not ceilings: a request settles against the compute it actually
    used, so a short question lands on its figure and a long build climbs past it. In practice the
    free allowance is a solid daily session — {sessionShape}, or, far more usually, a mix.
  </p>

  {/*
    TWO SENTENCES WERE REMOVED FROM THIS SECTION, AND BOTH WERE GUARANTEES NOBODY IMPLEMENTS.

    "If Apple estimates a run will be unusually expensive, it says so before starting instead of
    silently draining your day." There is no pre-run estimate anywhere a customer can see: the wire
    protocol has no cost-warning message, a run is admitted on a one-Credit spend whose only refusal
    is a zero balance, and the worker's own preferences.ts downgrades the `ask` tool permission to
    `deny` because "nothing in this product can interrupt a run to ask". The paragraph above already
    describes the live running total truthfully and stands on its own; a warning BEFORE the spend is
    a different promise, and it was not kept.

    "If a request fails on our side — an infrastructure error, not a build that turned out wrong —
    the Credits come back automatically." Nothing comes back. QuotaDO has no refund route at all —
    /spend clamps its amount with `Math.max(0, amount)` so a negative cannot be spent, and
    /grant-credits is "additive only, and never negative" and is reachable only from the Stripe
    webhook and an admin route. What is true is narrower and is now what this says: a call that
    failed reports no compute, so it is not charged in the first place. The one Credit taken at the
    start of the request is not returned, which is why that is said out loud rather than left for a
    reader to discover on the usage chart.
  */}
  <h2>How metering works</h2>
  <p>
    Your balance is always visible in the workspace header, and it updates live. One Credit is taken
    when a request starts, and the rest settles against the compute the run actually consumed — so
    the number moves while a long build is working, rather than arriving as a total you could not
    see coming.
  </p>
  <p>
    A step that fails on our side is not charged: Credits are settled from the compute a call
    actually reported, and a call that returned nothing reports nothing. The one Credit taken when
    the request started is not given back, and there is no other refund — a run that stops partway
    keeps whatever the finished steps cost.
  </p>

  <h2>The daily reset — and the monthly ceiling behind it</h2>
  <p>
    Quotas reset at midnight UTC — one fixed instant for every account, not a rolling window
    that starts when you do. That is worth knowing if you build late in the UTC day: credits
    spent at 23:00 UTC are refilled an hour later. The workspace shows your exact
    reset time. Unused Credits do not roll over — every day starts at your full allowance.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>There are two limits, and you have whichever is smaller.</strong> The daily figure is a
    rate; the monthly figure is a ceiling, and a day's refill cannot take you past it.
    {monthlyCeilingBitesFirst('free') ? (
      <>
        {' '}On the Free plan the ceiling is the one that bites: {free.creditsPerDay} a day against{' '}
        {free.creditsPerMonth.toLocaleString()} a month is <strong>{fullRateDays('free')} full days</strong>.
        Spend the whole daily allowance every day and the eleventh is where it stops until the month
        turns. Pace it and the same total stretches across the month instead.
      </>
    ) : (
      <>{' '}On every current plan the monthly ceiling clears a full month, so the daily figure is the
        one you will meet in practice.</>
    )}
    {' '}The usage meter names which of the two you are up against rather than showing one number for
    both.
  </p>

  <h2>Running out</h2>
  <p>When your balance hits zero:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>New AI requests are declined with a clear message and your reset time — never a silent failure.</li>
    <li>Everything that does not burn inference keeps working: reading chat history, browsing your project, creating and restoring checkpoints, pairing Studio.</li>
    <li>Nothing is deleted, downgraded or lost. You are just out of energy until the reset.</li>
  </ul>

  {/*
    THE SHARED CEILING WAS NOWHERE ON THIS PAGE, AND /docs/troubleshooting SENDS READERS HERE FOR IT.

    That page's capacity paragraph ends "See Credits & limits for the difference between the two" —
    and there was no second limit here to be different from. A customer met the service-wide wall
    for the first time as an error message, having read the page that was supposed to have told
    them. Described without a figure on purpose: the ceiling lives in apps/worker/src/pricing.ts and
    is the owner's to move, and a number copied onto a marketing page is a number free to drift.
    What does not drift is that the limit exists, is shared, and is not the reader's allowance.
  */}
  <h2>The limit that is not yours</h2>
  <p>
    Your allowance is not the only ceiling. Apple also caps what the <em>whole service</em> spends
    on inference in a day, shared by everyone using it, and that cap can be reached while your own
    balance still has Credits in it. A run stopped by it says so in its own words — “Apple has
    reached today's shared building capacity” — and it resets at midnight UTC like everything else
    here. It exists so that one runaway build cannot take the free tier down for the rest of the
    day. <a href="/docs/troubleshooting">Troubleshooting</a> covers what a run that hits it costs
    you and what it leaves behind.
  </p>

  <h2>Project files</h2>
  <p>
    Apple keeps notes, plans and generated data in a workspace beside each project — Apple's own
    storage, not your Roblox place — and you can open, add, rename and download those files from the
    Files drawer in the workspace. It holds <strong>text files only</strong>: .md, .txt, .json,
    .csv, .luau, .lua, .ts, .js, .yml and .yaml. Generated images are separate: download them
    from their result in the conversation. Roblox models live in your place.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>48 KB per file.</strong> A file past that is refused rather than truncated, so a
      half-saved document is never what you find later. Long material belongs in several files.</li>
    <li><strong>20 earlier versions of each file are kept.</strong> Every write archives the one
      before it, and any kept version can be read and put back — putting one back is itself a new
      version, so nothing in between is lost.</li>
    <li><strong>Deleted files are recoverable for 30 days.</strong> Delete moves a file to the
      project's trash with a real deadline on it, and the drawer says how long each one has left.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    None of this counts against your Credits. Credits pay for inference; files are storage, and the
    storage has the limits described here.
  </p>

  <h2>Generated images</h2>
  <p>
    New generated images stay with their project until it is deleted. Each image can be up to
    {GENERATED_IMAGE_MAX_BYTES / 1024 / 1024} MiB, with at most {GENERATED_PROJECT_COUNT} images
    and {GENERATED_PROJECT_BYTES / 1024 / 1024} MiB of encoded image data per project
    (encoding uses about a third more space than the downloaded file).
    Download a copy to keep outside Apple. Older temporary images that have expired cannot be recovered.
  </p>
  <p>
    Generation is refused before starting when project or service storage is full. Existing images
    are not removed to make room. Concurrent runs can still fill the space while an image is being
    generated; if saving fails, Apple reports that failure instead of claiming delivery.
  </p>

  <h2>Other limits worth knowing</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Rate limiting:</strong> requests are queued per account. Under heavy load, free-tier requests may briefly wait; Pro (when it ships) queues ahead.</li>
    <li><strong>Checkpoints:</strong> Apple keeps your 25 most recent checkpoints per project. Taking the twenty-sixth deletes the oldest in the same write — by age, whether you took it yourself or Apple took it before a change. Restore the one you want before it falls off the end.</li>
    <li><strong>One account per person:</strong> multiplying free accounts to farm quota is against the <a href="/terms">terms</a> and gets caught.</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="callout">
    <p>
      <strong>Making Credits go further:</strong> {thrift}. Nothing else moves the number much —
      the expensive request is almost always the one that had to be sent twice.
    </p>
  </div>
</DocsLayout>
